          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What's the most durable sign material for Florida weather?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Aluminum and ACM (aluminum composite) are the most durable options for Florida's sun, humidity, and storm exposure — both are rust-proof, warp-proof, and hold their finish for 7 to 10-plus years outdoors. Coroplast and vinyl banner are durable in the sense that the material itself won't fail structurally, but the printed graphics fade within 1 to 3 years in direct Florida sun, which is why they're the right choice for temporary or short-term signage rather than a permanent installation." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Can I use the same material indoors and outdoors?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Some materials cross over, others don't. Aluminum and ACM work equally well indoors and outdoors. Coroplast and vinyl banner work in both settings too, though they're most commonly used outdoors. Acrylic is the exception — it's primarily an indoor material because standard acrylic yellows and becomes brittle in direct Florida sun; it only goes outdoors when UV-stabilized and typically in a covered or shaded location." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What material should I use for a temporary real estate or event sign?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Corrugated plastic (coroplast) is the standard choice — it's inexpensive, lightweight, ready same-day or next-day, and holds up fine for the weeks or months a real estate listing, open house, or event actually needs signage. For something larger, like a sale banner across a storefront or fence, a vinyl banner is the better fit." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
