  <div class="page-hero" style="background-image: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(0,0,0,0.95) 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0.75) 60%, rgba(0,0,0,0.45) 100%), linear-gradient(to top, rgba(0,0,0,0.95) 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0.5) 40%, transparent 70%), url('/get%20right%20smoke%20shop%20corrugated%20sign%20and%20yard%20signs.JPEG'); background-size: cover; background-position: center;">
    <div class="page-hero-content">
      <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/" style="color:var(--grey);">Home</a> <span>/</span> <a href="/guides/" style="color:var(--grey);">Guides</a> <span>/</span> Sign Materials Guide</div>
      <h1>SIGN MATERIALS<br><span>COMPLETE GUIDE</span></h1>
      <p>Coroplast, vinyl, aluminum, ACM, or acrylic — which sign material actually fits your job, your budget, and Florida's climate. Written by a Pasco County sign shop that fabricates all of them in-house.</p>
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/contact/">Get a Free Quote</a>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:0.78rem;color:var(--grey);">Last updated: October 6, 2026 · 9 minute read</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <div class="section-label">Definitive Intro</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem);margin-bottom:1.2rem;">Why Sign Material Is the First Decision, Not the Last</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1.2rem;">Most people start a sign project by picking a size and a design. The material question gets asked last, or not at all — and that's backwards. The material you choose determines how long the sign lasts outdoors in Florida, whether it can go outdoors at all, how much it weighs on a wall or a stake, and how it gets mounted. Get the material wrong and you'll be replacing a sign that should have lasted years in a matter of months.</p>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1.2rem;">There's no single "best" sign material — a material that's perfect for a real estate yard sign (cheap, disposable, fast) would be a poor choice for a building-mounted business sign (needs years of outdoor durability), and a material that's ideal for an elegant lobby sign (acrylic) would yellow and degrade within a season if mounted outdoors in direct Pasco County sun.</p>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1.2rem;">This guide compares the five materials Fly Visuals fabricates most often for Tampa Bay and Pasco County customers — corrugated plastic, vinyl banner, aluminum, aluminum composite (ACM), and acrylic — with honest lifespan expectations for Florida's sun and humidity, not manufacturer marketing numbers.</p>

      <div style="background:var(--dark2);border-left:3px solid var(--red);padding:1.5rem 1.8rem;margin:2rem 0;">
        <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.8rem;">TL;DR Recommendation</div>
        <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;margin:0;padding-left:1.4rem;">
          <li><strong style="color:var(--white);">Need it up today or this week, and it's temporary?</strong> Corrugated plastic (coroplast) — yard signs, job site signs, real estate signs, event signage.</li>
          <li><strong style="color:var(--white);">Promoting an event, sale, or grand opening?</strong> Vinyl banner — fast turnaround, hangs almost anywhere, affordable at large sizes.</li>
          <li><strong style="color:var(--white);">Need it outdoors for years with zero maintenance?</strong> Aluminum or ACM — rust-proof, warp-proof, holds up to Florida's full climate.</li>
          <li><strong style="color:var(--white);">Need a premium look indoors — lobby, office, medical, retail?</strong> Acrylic — clear, frosted, or backlit for a finished, professional feel.</li>
          <li><strong style="color:var(--white);">Need it to glow or be visible after dark?</strong> Backlit acrylic (indoor) or illuminated channel letters (outdoor) — and plan for an electrical permit; see our <a href="/guides/sign-permitting-guide/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;">Sign Permitting Guide</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
      <div class="section-label">At a Glance</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem);margin-bottom:1.2rem;">Sign Materials Compared</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.6;min-width:760px;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background:var(--dark);border-bottom:2px solid var(--red);">
              <th style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;text-align:left;color:var(--white);font-family:var(--font-display);letter-spacing:0.05em;">Material</th>
              <th style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;text-align:left;color:var(--white);font-family:var(--font-display);letter-spacing:0.05em;">Indoor / Outdoor</th>
              <th style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;text-align:left;color:var(--white);font-family:var(--font-display);letter-spacing:0.05em;">FL Lifespan</th>
              <th style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;text-align:left;color:var(--white);font-family:var(--font-display);letter-spacing:0.05em;">Typical Turnaround</th>
              <th style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;text-align:left;color:var(--white);font-family:var(--font-display);letter-spacing:0.05em;">Best For</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--grey-dark);">
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;color:var(--white);font-weight:600;">Corrugated Plastic (Coroplast)</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Outdoor (short-term) &amp; indoor</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">1&ndash;2 years</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Same day / next day</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Yard signs, job sites, events</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--grey-dark);background:var(--dark);">
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;color:var(--white);font-weight:600;">Vinyl Banner</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Outdoor &amp; indoor</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">1&ndash;3 years</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">2&ndash;3 business days</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Events, promotions, grand openings</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--grey-dark);">
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;color:var(--white);font-weight:600;">Aluminum (Flat Panel)</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Outdoor &amp; indoor</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">7&ndash;10+ years</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Quoted per job</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Property signs, long-term outdoor signage</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--grey-dark);background:var(--dark);">
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;color:var(--white);font-weight:600;">ACM (Aluminum Composite)</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Outdoor &amp; indoor</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">7&ndash;10+ years</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Quoted per job</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Large storefront &amp; building-mounted panels</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;color:var(--white);font-weight:600;">Acrylic</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Indoor (primarily)</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Years indoors; limited outdoors</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Quoted per job</td>
              <td style="padding:0.9rem 0.8rem;">Lobbies, offices, medical, retail</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--grey);font-size:0.82rem;line-height:1.7;margin-top:1.2rem;">Lifespans reflect real-world Pasco County / Tampa Bay sun, humidity, and storm exposure, not laboratory ratings. Pricing for every material depends on size, thickness, quantity, and finish, so we quote every job individually rather than publishing a flat number — same as most sign shops.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.8vw,2rem);margin-bottom:1rem;">Corrugated Plastic (Coroplast)</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1rem;">Corrugated plastic — also called coroplast or corex — is a fluted plastic sheet, typically 4mm thick, printed with full-color UV-resistant outdoor inks. It's the material behind nearly every yard sign, job site sign, and real estate sign you see driving through Pasco County, and for good reason: it's lightweight, inexpensive, and ready same-day or next-day.</p>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div>
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:#5fb35f;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Pros</div>
          <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;padding-left:1.2rem;margin:0;">
            <li>Lowest cost sign material by far</li>
            <li>Same day / next day turnaround available</li>
            <li>Lightweight — easy to stake, zip-tie, or mount</li>
            <li>Structurally durable — won't rot, rust, or warp</li>
            <li>Any quantity from 1 to 500+</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:#cc1414;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Cons</div>
          <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;padding-left:1.2rem;margin:0;">
            <li>Print fades after about a year in direct Florida sun, especially south/west exposures</li>
            <li>Catches wind on large sizes — not ideal in high-wind, coastal-exposed locations</li>
            <li>Not the right choice for anything meant to stay up for years</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Best for:</strong> Real estate signs, contractor job site signs, political and event signs, open house and directional signs, retail promotions, and any short-term need where speed and low cost matter more than multi-year durability.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.8vw,2rem);margin-bottom:1rem;">Vinyl Banner</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1rem;">Vinyl banners are flexible, printed vinyl sheets finished with reinforced hems and brass grommets for hanging. We print on 13 oz standard vinyl for most jobs or 18 oz heavy-duty vinyl when a banner needs to hold up longer or in rougher conditions, and mesh vinyl for high-wind locations — a construction fence, a chain-link run along a busy road, or anywhere near the Gulf where a solid banner would catch too much wind.</p>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div>
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:#5fb35f;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Pros</div>
          <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;padding-left:1.2rem;margin:0;">
            <li>Affordable even at very large sizes</li>
            <li>Fast — most banners ready in 2 to 3 business days, rush orders available</li>
            <li>Hangs almost anywhere — fence, wall, truss, between poles</li>
            <li>Mesh option available for high-wind locations</li>
            <li>Works indoors and outdoors</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:#cc1414;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Cons</div>
          <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;padding-left:1.2rem;margin:0;">
            <li>Not a permanent solution — 1 to 3 years outdoors depending on sun/wind exposure</li>
            <li>A solid (non-mesh) banner in a high-wind spot is prone to tearing at grommets</li>
            <li>Needs a structure to hang from — not freestanding</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Best for:</strong> Grand openings, sales and promotions, trade shows (often on a retractable stand), fundraisers and community events, and any outdoor advertising need where you want the biggest visual impact per dollar.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.8vw,2rem);margin-bottom:1rem;">Aluminum &amp; ACM (Aluminum Composite)</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1rem;">When a sign needs to survive years outside in Florida without fading, warping, rusting, or cracking, aluminum is the material the sign industry has relied on for generations. Flat panel aluminum is solid aluminum sheet — typically .040" or .063" thick — cut to size and finished with digital print or vinyl application. ACM (aluminum composite material) is two thin aluminum sheets bonded to a polyethylene core: lighter than solid aluminum, very rigid, and highly resistant to warping even across large panel sizes, which is why it's the standard for big storefront and building-mounted panels.</p>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div>
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:#5fb35f;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Pros</div>
          <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;padding-left:1.2rem;margin:0;">
            <li>Doesn't rust, warp, or crack — looks sharp years after installation</li>
            <li>UV-stable finish holds color far longer than plastic substrates</li>
            <li>Any size, from a small property sign to a large commercial panel</li>
            <li>Digital print or vinyl application, depending on the design</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:#cc1414;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Cons</div>
          <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.7;padding-left:1.2rem;margin:0;">
            <li>Higher upfront cost than coroplast or banner vinyl</li>
            <li>Heavier than ACM at large sizes — needs real mounting hardware, not stakes</li>
            <li>A new permanent wall or monument-mounted sign usually needs a permit — see our <a href="/guides/sign-permitting-guide/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;">Sign Permitting Guide</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Best for:</strong> Permanent property and building signage, post-and-panel signs, storefront panels, facility and wayfinding signs, and any outdoor sign meant to be a multi-year investment rather than a recurring reprint.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.8vw,2rem);margin-bottom:1rem;">Acrylic</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1rem;">Acrylic is the material for signs meant to be seen up close, not just driven past. Clear, frosted, or colored acrylic panels — flat-cut, layered for dimension, or backlit with edge-lit LEDs — give lobbies, offices, medical practices, and retail spaces a polished, professional look that a printed substrate sign can't match at the same price point.</p>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1rem;">Acrylic's one real limitation: it's primarily an indoor material in Florida. Standard acrylic yellows and becomes brittle under direct sun over time. For an outdoor-facing application, that means either UV-stabilized acrylic in a covered or shaded spot, or switching to aluminum or ACM instead — we'll tell you honestly which is the right call for your specific location.</p>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Best for:</strong> Reception and lobby signs, office suite identification, medical and professional offices, retail displays, and any indoor space where the sign itself should read as premium, not just functional.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <div class="section-label">Matching Material to Sign Type</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem);margin-bottom:1.2rem;">Which Sign Type Uses Which Material</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1.2rem;">If you already know what kind of sign you need, the material is usually already decided for you. Here's how it maps to the sign types we fabricate most often for Pasco County businesses:</p>
      <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;padding-left:1.4rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;"><a href="/yard-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">Yard signs</a> and <a href="/corrugated-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">corrugated plastic signs</a> &mdash; coroplast, nearly always.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;"><a href="/vinyl-banners/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">Vinyl banners</a> &mdash; 13oz, 18oz, or mesh vinyl depending on location and wind exposure.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;"><a href="/aluminum-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">Aluminum signs</a> and most <a href="/outdoor-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">outdoor signs</a> &mdash; flat panel aluminum or ACM.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;"><a href="/acrylic-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">Acrylic signs</a> and most <a href="/indoor-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">indoor signs</a> &mdash; acrylic, in clear, frosted, colored, or backlit form.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;"><a href="/business-signs/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600;">Business signs</a> (storefront, monument, pole, channel letters) &mdash; usually aluminum or ACM, often illuminated. Illuminated signs need an electrical permit; see our <a href="/guides/sign-permitting-guide/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;">Sign Permitting Guide</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem);margin-bottom:1.2rem;">Mistakes That Cost Pasco County Customers Money</h2>
      <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;padding-left:1.4rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Putting standard acrylic outdoors.</strong> It yellows and becomes brittle in Florida sun within a season. If it has to go outside, it needs to be UV-stabilized acrylic, or it needs to be a different material entirely.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Choosing a solid vinyl banner for a high-wind spot.</strong> A banner on a construction fence or anywhere exposed near the Gulf should be mesh, not solid vinyl — solid vinyl catches wind and tears at the grommets.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Expecting coroplast to last like aluminum.</strong> It's a fantastic material for its price point, but it's built for months to a couple of years outdoors, not a decade. If a sign needs to stay up for years, budget for aluminum or ACM from the start instead of reprinting coroplast repeatedly.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Not asking about mounting before ordering a large panel.</strong> ACM and aluminum panels get heavy at large sizes. Confirm the mounting method (wall-mounted, post-and-panel, standoffs) before the sign is fabricated, not after.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Assuming any permanent outdoor sign is permit-free.</strong> A new wall-mounted, monument, or pole sign typically needs a permit in Pasco, Pinellas, and Hillsborough County — see our <a href="/guides/sign-permitting-guide/" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;">Sign Permitting Guide</a> before you order fabrication.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <div class="section-label">Frequently Asked</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem);margin-bottom:2rem;">Sign Material Questions (Pasco County / Tampa Bay)</h2>
      <div class="faq-accordion">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-question-text">What's the most durable sign material for Florida weather?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Aluminum and ACM (aluminum composite) are the most durable options for Florida's sun, humidity, and storm exposure — both are rust-proof, warp-proof, and hold their finish for 7 to 10-plus years outdoors. Coroplast and vinyl banner are durable in the sense that the material itself won't fail structurally, but the printed graphics fade within 1 to 3 years in direct Florida sun, which is why they're the right choice for temporary or short-term signage rather than a permanent installation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-question-text">Can I use the same material indoors and outdoors?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Some materials cross over, others don't. Aluminum and ACM work equally well indoors and outdoors. Coroplast and vinyl banner work in both settings too, though they're most commonly used outdoors. Acrylic is the exception — it's primarily an indoor material because standard acrylic yellows and becomes brittle in direct Florida sun; it only goes outdoors when UV-stabilized and typically in a covered or shaded location.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-question-text">What material should I use for a temporary real estate or event sign?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Corrugated plastic (coroplast) is the standard choice — it's inexpensive, lightweight, ready same-day or next-day, and holds up fine for the weeks or months a real estate listing, open house, or event actually needs signage. For something larger, like a sale banner across a storefront or fence, a vinyl banner is the better fit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-question-text">Does an illuminated sign cost more than a standard sign?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Yes — illuminated signs (backlit acrylic, channel letters) cost more up front because they include an electrical component and, almost always, a separate electrical permit on top of the standard sign permit. They're worth it for a storefront that needs to be visible after dark, but budget for both the fabrication and the permitting when you're planning one. Our <a href="/guides/sign-permitting-guide/" style="color:var(--red);text-decoration:underline;">Sign Permitting Guide</a> covers what's involved.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="faq-question-text">How much does each sign material cost?</span>
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>It depends on size, thickness, quantity, and finish for every material — which is why we quote each job individually rather than publishing a flat rate, the same way most sign shops operate. As a general rule of thumb, coroplast is the most affordable, vinyl banners are affordable even at large sizes, and aluminum, ACM, and acrylic cost more up front but deliver a longer-lasting or more premium result. Call (727) 494-6162 or request a quote online for a real number on your specific project.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content-block alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;">
      <div class="section-label">Where to Get One</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem);margin-bottom:1.2rem;">Fly Visuals &mdash; Pasco County &amp; Tampa Bay Sign Shop</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:1rem;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:1rem;">Fly Visuals designs, fabricates, and installs signs in every material covered in this guide from our shop at 8132 Cormel St in Port Richey, FL — serving businesses across Pasco, Pinellas, and Hillsborough County.</p>
      <ul style="color:var(--grey-light);font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.85;padding-left:1.4rem;margin-bottom:1.2rem;">
        <li>In-house design, fabrication, and installation &mdash; no outsourcing</li>
        <li>Coroplast, vinyl banner, aluminum, ACM, and acrylic all fabricated on-site</li>
        <li>1-year labor warranty, plus the manufacturer material warranty passed through to you</li>
        <li>Licensed &amp; insured &mdash; general liability coverage</li>
        <li>We handle permit submission on jobs that need one, billed at cost with no markup</li>
